WEBVTT

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.040
Hey, Motion, my name’s Skyler,

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.100
and I’m making this from
Kelowna, BC, Canada.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.090
I spent the last couple years working
in technical product support,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.400
where I really enjoy figuring out
what isn’t working,

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.620
getting to the bottom of it,
and helping people understand it,

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.200
become more confident
with the technology that they’re using.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.620
What interests me about Motion

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.600
is the combination of technical
troubleshooting, AI, and creativity.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.170
Outside of work, I spend a lot
of my free time

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.500
exploring, creating
AI-assisted projects.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.750
I recently created an AI-assisted
short film called Tokyo Through Time.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.900
So the creative side of Motion’s
platform generally excites me.

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:40.680
I think it’d be exciting and impactful
to help people understand

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.200
how their creative work is performing

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.500
and while learning the technology
behind it.

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:49.460
As for hard feedback to hear,
one that really stuck out with me

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.380
is I can sometimes become
a little too technical

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.600
when explaining escalations
to internal partners

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.500
and external support partners.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.450
What was hard to hear about this
is that I thought that

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.040
being so thorough was providing
all the information they needed

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:07.780
and reducing unnecessary back-and-forth,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.500
speeding up resolution time.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.510
But after reflecting, I realized
that not everyone understands

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.950
the terminology and workflows
as well as I do.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.500
So I started to change my approach
to these conversations.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.910
I would lead with what the issue is,
what we’ve done,

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:23.950
and what we need to do next,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
and only include the technical details
when necessary.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.100
Very similar to how I go about
these issues with clients.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.730
That made me realize that
communicating effectively

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.080
and being technically accurate
aren’t always the same thing.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.400
You really have to adopt
who you’re speaking to.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.600
Anyways, thank you for taking the time
for watching this application video.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.620
I look forward to learning more
about Motion and the team.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:45.860
Thanks.
